## 1. Governing standard: Reg BI and FINRA suitability principles

Because this was a brokerage account and Thomas made a securities recommendation to a retail customer in October 2022, SEC Regulation Best Interest applies. Reg BI requires the broker/advisor, when making a recommendation, to act in the retail customer’s best interest and not place the broker’s or firm’s interests ahead of the customer’s.

The key obligation here is the Reg BI “Care Obligation,” which requires the broker to exercise reasonable diligence, care, and skill to:

1. Understand the potential risks, rewards, and costs of the recommendation;
2. Have a reasonable basis to believe the recommendation is in the customer’s best interest based on the customer’s investment profile; and
3. Have a reasonable basis to believe the recommendation does not put the broker’s interests ahead of the customer’s.

FINRA Rule 2111 suitability concepts remain highly relevant. The customer investment profile includes, among other things:

- Age;
- Income;
- Net worth and liquid net worth;
- Investment experience;
- Investment objective;
- Risk tolerance;
- Time horizon;
- Liquidity needs;
- Financial situation and needs;
- Tax status; and
- Other relevant information disclosed by the customer.

## 2. Thomas could not simply rely on the stale January 2022 profile

When Thomas received the transferred account in August 2022, he could see the original documentation. Standing alone, merely receiving the account and not immediately re-verifying every item may not itself be a suitability violation if no recommendation was being made.

But once Gerald called in October 2022 and disclosed major changes, Thomas had a duty to investigate and update the relevant customer profile information before making a recommendation.

